# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b5), edited. sister_rain's first conversation.

~ start
set sister_rain_met
Narrator: The silver sunflower on the preacher's chest catches the bonfire light as she turns, her red hair whipping around her shoulders like autumn leaves.
sister_rain [smile]: Blessings of the gentle rain upon your boots, strangers! Come warm your hands and hear how the Patient Lady keeps the seed safe beneath the frost!
-> menu

~ menu
* Ask about the faith of the Patient Lady -> about_work
* Ask what troubles her about Wickermoor -> about_village
* Ask about the wider valley -> rumour
* [Nature DC 13] Watch how she presses her bare palm to the cobbles -> check_ok | check_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ about_work
sister_rain [smile]: The earth doesn't ask for heads on an oak or cages of iron, friends, it only asks for patience and clean rain.
sister_rain [angry]: Yet folk here meet every shadow with an axe and a torch, fighting fire with fire until the whole blessed meadow is ash!
-> menu

~ about_village
sister_rain [weary]: Mayor Somerton means well enough in his stiff way, I suppose, though his Old Ways have grown sour and hungry. Then next moment my heart just sings to see a green shoot cracking the stone!
-> menu

~ rumour
sister_rain [sad]: If your feet carry you down the road to Foxwillow, go with a soft word on your tongue. Neighbour has turned against neighbour down there, and their bitter blood is poisoning the creek.
-> menu

~ check_ok
Narrator: Where her fingers brush the mud between the stones, a shudder runs up her arm as though she touched a hot kettle, and the hem of her green dress wilts grey.
sister_rain [afraid]: Can you not feel it stinging through your soles? Something buried deep under the roots is festering like a rusty nail, and the whole valley's soil is sick with fever.
-> menu

~ check_fail
Narrator: She rises from the mud and dusts her skirt with a sudden, bright laugh, the storm in her green eyes passing as quick as an April shower.
sister_rain [smile]: Only checking if the dew has fallen yet. A country girl never trusts the sky without feeling the dirt first.
-> menu
